WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.020
Multi-agent orchestration now delivers measurable gains in long horizon reliability by letting sub-agents

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:13.340
request harness changes on the fly. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 202. A new RxIV paper calls

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:19.240
that system P-Orchestra. Sub-agents can ask the orchestrator for a harness retune at any logical

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:25.160
step. Local execution keeps going while the request is handled. Across three long horizon

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.820
benchmarks, tasks success rose 9.1% over the strongest baseline.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:35.680
Pair that with on-policy orchestration distillation, a training method for the controller, and

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.680
small orchestrator success rose 10% over earlier training baselines.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.960
The paper also reports a more favorable cost performance trade-off.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.460
Meta released new AI models that the company says matched the industry's best, tech-gig

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:49.940
reports.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.640
Those models will power its Mew's agent.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.040
They aim at competitive performance in agentic settings.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.720
In those settings, orchestration and tool use matter more than raw scale alone.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.880
They give another closed-weight option to benchmark against the Claude and GPT families.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.560
Wider availability and the integration details that come with it is still ahead.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:18.380
A pre-registered RXIV study ran three prompt optimizers, GEPA, MEPRO V2, and Texgrad,

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:25.660
on hotpot QA, squad 2.0, and IFE Val. All three produce regressions on unscored

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.520
requirements such as citation validity and output format. Those breaks appeared

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:36.260
when the output token cap stayed at 200. Lifting the cap to 1,024 tokens removed

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:41.400
extraction failures. Content regressions stayed below one point and exact match

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.360
improved further. Prompt optimization can raise accuracy while silently violating

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:51.680
explicit output contracts. A separate RxIV paper uses a label-free procedure

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.720
that rewrites only the most contested rubric clauses. Interjudge Krippendorf's

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:03.300
Alpha, a consensus score, rose 0.069 on Somival. The same edits produce no

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.560
detectable change in agreement with expert human ratings. The effect did not

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:16.340
meet pre-registered criteria on health bench or USR topical chat. Raising

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:22.200
And RxIV paper on host-aware crawling finds that roughly half the variation in document

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.640
quality classifier scores sits between hosts rather than within them.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.000
Pull a handful of pages from each host, then crawl deeper on the hosts that score well,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:38.520
and you gather several times more high quality pages than uniform depth crawls or centrality-based allocation.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.580
Common crawls observed per host coverage showed little correlation with evaluated quality signals.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:50.460
The authors estimate tens of millions of high-quality pages remain among known but uncrawled

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:50.880
URLs.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.820
MoPROSD, also on RXIV, splits prompts into functional semantic modules.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.660
It refines those modules with class-conditioned large-language model or LLM feedback.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:09.560
On semival 2016, class-conditioned feedback beat pooled feedback for four of five backbones.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:16.160
The framework introduces vaccine stance, a new human annotated benchmark for vaccine stance detection.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:22.080
Both reasoning-based refinement strategies stayed competitive with recent prompt optimization methods.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.200
That held across four datasets.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:30.800
Basis Bench on RxIV evaluates eight recent speech LLMs on demographic robustness,

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.120
stereotypical bias and explicit personalization.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.000
Most models recognize demographic cues.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.580
They did not turn those cues into appropriate personalization.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:44.160
Explicit demographic labels improved personalization.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:50.100
Those labels also amplified stereotypical associations already present in the textual backbones.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.560
The benchmark separates beneficial personalization from unprompted stereotypical behavior.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.480
NASCARM notes that single agent systems lack work decomposition, failure tolerance, and

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.940
real-time coordination once tasks become layered or unpredictable.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:10.280
multiple. Multi-agent setups run tasks in parallel. They tolerate individual failures.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.760
They retune coordination as conditions change. The payoff comes from better orchestration

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.020
rather than larger individual models. Mashreck deployed AI agents that cut integration

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:27.120
time by up to 70 percent, PYM and TS reports. The agents take on repetitive coordination

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.360
work that previously needed manual effort across systems. The deployment shows measurable

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:38.380
efficiency gains in a regulated financial environment, and Thropic presented dynamic workflow patterns

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:44.520
at AI Engineer New York, according to Layton Space. The approach borrows UNIX and Linux lessons

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:50.200
for managing agent coordination. It lets agents take on larger projects without flooding developers

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:57.520
with every intermediate state. It favors adaptive orchestration over static agent graphs. Python 3.15

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:04.480
ads explicit lazy imports via PEP 810, Simon Willison notes. The release includes a new sampling

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:11.560
profiler called Takion. It also adds unpacking syntax and comprehensions. Willison walk through upgrading

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:18.580
via UV and dropping Python 3.10, which is no longer maintained. The release enables cleaner plug-in

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:25.180
architectures. It also lowers startup costs for LLM command line tools. The GitHub blog shows how

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:31.280
to add custom instructions, skills, and model context protocol, or MCP, server connections

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:32.320
to a repository.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.480
That setup helps co-pilot understand project-specific patterns.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:41.000
The configuration lives at repository level and persists across sessions.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.000
Developers new to co-pilot can follow the steps to cut generic suggestions on their own

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:46.640
code bases.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.660
The guidance stays on practical setup rather than model internals.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.060
DryLab AI closed a $3 million seed round.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:06:00.720
the business times reports. Vertex Ventures and Pat Snap led the round. The platform focuses on

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:07.540
agent workflows for enterprise use cases. An RXIV survey of natural language processing or NLP

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:16.100
for Tibetan languages reviewed 140 publications from 2004 to 2026. 84% of that work appeared since

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:23.720
2018. The papers covered all 25 represented NLP research areas. Researchers based in China first

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:29.940
authored 87% of the work, only 29% of publications released data, code or models.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:36.340
The authors flag gaps in dialect coverage, native evaluation, and responsible NLP practices

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.020
for this low resource setting.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.340
Point the prompt optimizer study code at your own question answering tasks, and log

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.100
whether citations and output format still hold.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:53.100
Upgrade a personal LLM command line project to Python 3.15 with UV, and switch on explicit

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:58.820
at lazy imports plus the tackian profiler. Add repository-level custom instructions to

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:05.080
a GitHub co-pilot workspace and time how fast the generic suggestions drop off. More labs are

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:09.760
expected to release agent orchestration frameworks that support on-the-fly harness adjustments.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:16.380
Additional speech LLM benchmarks will likely appear that separate personalization from stereotypical bias.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:22.580
Open-weight Tibetan NLP resources are projected to grow as the surveyed community adopts shared

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:28.540
standards. Cheap episode keys are the piece I want to pop the hood on in T2Mem, a test

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:34.000
time memory system from a new RX-IV paper. The system refuses to pre-build a full memory

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:39.580
for every possible question. Offline, it stores only lightweight episode-level metadata and

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:45.040
keeps pointers back to the original interactions. Those records are episode keys, temporal links,

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:50.400
and raw interaction pointers sitting ready for later reconstruction. At inference, the model

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.900
Pulls a small set of those metadata items first.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:57.940
It then fetches and organizes the underlying turns only as reasoning moves forward.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:03.360
Query specific memory grows along the reasoning process instead of arriving as a finished blob.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:07.420
Construction adapts dynamically to each query's information needs.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:13.460
Experiments on low-como and long-mem evil show consistent gains across backbone LLMs.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:17.940
Memory construction overhead is substantially lower than building everything up front.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:24.120
You pay a modest increase in per query latency, you get a large cut in the always-on memory footprint.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:28.440
The quality gain is largest when the evidence you need sits in distant sessions.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:33.200
Once the context window already holds the entire history, that advantage shrinks.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:39.280
The failure mode that shows up first is retrieving two few metadata items for reconstruction to be reliable.

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:46.180
Use test time construction when average session length exceeds what fits comfortably in context and queries are sparse

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:50.660
and stick with offline memory when queries are frequent and latency budgets are tight.

